    "overview": "Semaglutide's beneficial effect on MASH liver disease https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zL8B4OQ9F7o [00:00:06] So this is a phase three trial of a drug [00:00:10] called simaglotide popularly known as [00:00:12] ompic [00:…",
    "body": "Semaglutide's beneficial effect on MASH liver disease\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zL8B4OQ9F7o\n\n[00:00:06] So this is a phase three trial of a drug\n[00:00:10] called simaglotide popularly known as\n[00:00:12] ompic\n[00:00:14] uh that is commonly used for patients\n[00:00:16] with diabetes and obesity.\n[00:00:19] The focus of this study was really to\n[00:00:21] see whether it affects one of the common\n[00:00:25] conditions that is associated with\n[00:00:27] obesity and diabetes which is fatty\n[00:00:30] liver disease particularly a form called\n[00:00:33] stato heepitis which can lead to\n[00:00:36] scarring of the liver and even cerosis\n[00:00:38] and liver\n[00:00:39] cancer. The study is divided into two\n[00:00:42] parts. an initial part where we look at\n[00:00:45] the liver under the microscope and see\n[00:00:47] whether things are beginning to look\n[00:00:49] better and the second part is when we\n[00:00:51] continue to follow patients to see if\n[00:00:54] actual patient outcomes are\n[00:00:56] improved. So the stud study report that\n[00:00:59] we will present really focuses on the\n[00:01:02] first part where we show that the liver\n[00:01:06] actually starts looking substantially\n[00:01:08] better under the microscope in these\n[00:01:11] patients. So this is a study population\n[00:01:14] of people who already had started\n[00:01:16] scarring down their liver and what we\n[00:01:19] show is that this drug can very\n[00:01:21] effectively reverse the scarring as well\n[00:01:25] as the fat and injury in the liver. So\n[00:01:28] this is incredibly exciting news because\n[00:01:31] many of our patients who have this\n[00:01:33] condition are also obese and have\n[00:01:35] diabetes and are at risk for heart\n[00:01:37] disease as well. So this molecule, this\n[00:01:40] drug has already been shown to be\n[00:01:42] beneficial for obesity, diabetes, heart\n[00:01:45] disease, etc. Now we can add liver\n[00:01:48] disease to that list. So imagine one\n[00:01:52] drug to treat all of these different\n[00:01:54] conditions. So this is a major step\n[00:01:57] forward in the field and it provides\n[00:01:59] patients additional choices when we\n[00:02:03] think about treating fatty liver\n[00:02:05] disease.\n[00:02:08] The studies for approval of for this\n[00:02:12] particular disease are\n[00:02:14] generally considered in two time frames.\n[00:02:18] First in the short time frame to see if\n[00:02:21] the liver starts looking better under\n[00:02:23] the microscope because that occurs\n[00:02:24] before actually patient outcomes\n[00:02:28] occur. Nash as this condition is called\n[00:02:32] uh progresses over many years before\n[00:02:35] people get cerosis and they get cerosis\n[00:02:38] related complications which often\n[00:02:40] require a\n[00:02:42] transplant. In the first part with\n[00:02:45] within a year or so if we can show that\n[00:02:48] the liver is looking better then that\n[00:02:50] dramatically increases the likelihood\n[00:02:52] that this will translate into better\n[00:02:55] patient outcomes.\n[00:02:57] So the approval of these type of drugs\n[00:03:00] is contingent on demonstrating in this\n[00:03:03] early\n[00:03:04] phase a pre-specified amount of\n[00:03:07] improvement in what the liver looks like\n[00:03:10] under the microscope and we exceeded\n[00:03:13] that threshold. So this is very exciting\n[00:03:15] for people like me who spent 20 30 years\n[00:03:18] taking care of patients with this\n[00:03:20] condition. This is hope and this gives\n[00:03:24] us why we've been doing this for so\n[00:03:26] long. It's incredibly gratifying to see\n[00:03:29] the results and to be able to have\n[00:03:32] something hopefully down the road that\n[00:03:35] will get approved and then we will be\n[00:03:38] able to offer this to all our patients\n[00:03:40] who need it.\n[00:03:43] Well, imagine\n[00:03:44] this that this condition perks along\n[00:03:48] silently and then one day it's suddenly\n[00:03:53] discovered that you have cerosis or even\n[00:03:55] worse you have cerosis with a\n[00:03:58] cancer. At that point you're suddenly\n[00:04:01] faced with a\n[00:04:02] lifechanging piece of\n[00:04:05] information and many of these patients\n[00:04:08] already have underlying heart disease\n[00:04:10] etc. which make them very high risk for\n[00:04:13] liver transplant. So transplant is a not\n[00:04:16] an option for many of these patients. So\n[00:04:19] few lucky people can be saved with a\n[00:04:21] liver transplant but for the rest it\n[00:04:24] becomes a death sentence. And what\n[00:04:27] studies like this and drugs like this\n[00:04:29] mean is that we can identify the\n[00:04:32] patients early, we can treat them early\n[00:04:35] and we can prevent that scenario in many\n[00:04:38] patients. maybe not in 100% but this is\n[00:04:41] a very active field. There a lot of\n[00:04:43] drugs coming behind this and they all\n[00:04:46] look very very promising and so we're\n[00:04:50] very very hopeful that we really think\n[00:04:52] we're at this point in time where we are\n[00:04:55] getting to that tipping point where for\n[00:04:57] the last 20 years we had almost nothing\n[00:04:59] to offer. Last year, as you know, the\n[00:05:01] first drug was conditionally approved.\n[00:05:03] It's called\n[00:05:04] ResMatter. And now we have very exciting\n[00:05:07] positive data from this study, which\n[00:05:10] hopefully will lead to a approval. Only\n[00:05:13] time will tell, but behind it are drugs\n[00:05:15] that are looking progressively better.\n[00:05:18] So, this is very very exciting for\n[00:05:20] everybody in this space.",
